303	}# Workflow format
304	
305	This page describes the workflow JSON that defines a Freya agent. Read it before you write a builder. Tools, API calls and code are in [tools-and-code.md](tools-and-code.md). The REST calls are in [api.md](api.md).
306	
307	Some statements are marked "verified in a working builder". Those come from a builder that created a 6-node workflow through the REST API and whose agent passed its practice cases.
308	
309	## What a workflow is
310	
311	A workflow describes a conversation as a graph. Each **node** is one step of the conversation and has its own prompt. **Transitions** move the conversation from one node to the next. A transition fires on what the user said or on the value of a variable.
312	
313	When you create a workflow through the API, the platform creates an agent for it. You run simulations against that agent.
314	
315	## Three model calls per turn
316	
317	After each user message the platform makes three separate model calls. They do not share a prompt.
318	
319	```text
320	user message
321	    |
322	    v
323	1. EXTRACTION   reads the message, stores variables
324	2. ROUTING      decides whether to leave the node
325	3. DIALOGUE     writes the agent's reply
326	```
327	
328	| Call | What it sees | What it produces |
329	|---|---|---|
330	| Extraction | Recent conversation messages and the current node's `extraction_schema` | Variable values |
331	| Routing | Recent conversation messages and the transition descriptions. It does not see the dialogue prompt | The transition to take, or none |
332	| Dialogue | `system_prompt`, `role_message`, `task_message`, the full conversation history, and the node's `tools` | The reply, or a tool call |
333	
334	Consequences:
335	
336	- A routing instruction inside `task_message` has no effect on routing. Routing reads only transitions.
337	- The dialogue model can call only the tools in the current node's `tools` array. Everything else (API calls on entry, code, ending the call) is run by the workflow engine.
338	- Deterministic conditions (variable checks) need no model call. They are evaluated in code.
339	
340	## Top-level keys
341	
342	```json
343	{
344	  "initial_node": "greeting",
345	  "system_prompt": "You are the assistant of a dental clinic. Speak English.",
346	  "nodes": {},
347	  "global_nodes": {},
348	  "test_variables": { "customer_id": "c_test" }
349	}
350	```
351	
352	| Key | Type | Required | Meaning |
353	|---|---|---|---|
354	| `initial_node` | string | yes | Key of the node where every call starts |
355	| `nodes` | object | yes | Map of node key to node definition |
356	| `system_prompt` | string | no | Global rules. Part of the dialogue prompt in every node |
357	| `global_nodes` | object | no | Interrupt handlers that can fire from any node. See "Global nodes" |
358	| `test_variables` | object | no | Default values for call variables. See "Variables" |
359	| `context_variables` | object | no | A state block of chosen variables for the dialogue model. See "Context variables" |
360	| `tools` | array | no | Workflow-level lifecycle tools. See tools-and-code.md |
361	| `agent_config` | object | no | Model and call settings. See below |
362	| `nodePositions` | object | no | Layout for the visual editor. The working builder omits it |
363	
364	**`agent_config`**: omit it. The platform fills in defaults. It holds model, voice and timeout settings that a builder for this event does not need. In a simulation you choose the models with the override fields described in api.md.
365	
366	**No `null` values.** The schema is strict. Leave an optional field out, or set an optional string to `""`. A `null` fails validation.
367	
368	The REST API takes the workflow inside a request body `{"name": "...", "workflow": {...}}`. The object described on this page is the value of `workflow`.
369	
370	## Node types
371	
372	| `node_type` | Purpose | Speaks to the user | Carries |
373	|---|---|---|---|
374	| `conversation` | A dialogue step | yes | `task_message`, `tools`, `extraction_schema`, `transitions` |
375	| `api_request` | Runs an HTTP call on entry | usually not | `pre_actions`, node-level `response_mapping`, `transitions` |
376	| `code` | Runs a Python snippet on entry | usually not | `pre_actions` with a `code_exec` function, `transitions` |
377	| `end_call` | Ends the call | optional farewell | `terminal` |
378	
379	Other node types exist: `transfer_call`, `integration`, `knowledge_base`, `reschedule_call`, `invoke_agent`, `exit`. Telephony, voice settings, knowledge base and integrations also exist on the platform. You do not need any of them here and this page does not describe them.
